<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Lüneburg</h1><p class="st-meta">1269 CE – 1705 CE · capital Celle</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Guelph principality of the Lüneburg Heath ruled from Celle, which embraced the Reformation early and merged into the Electorate of Hanover in 1705.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1269 the brothers Albert and John divided the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-brunswick-luneburg">Duchy of Brunswick-Lüneburg</a>, which their father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-the-child">Otto the Child</a> had received in 1235. John received the northern lands around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celle">Celle</a>, founding the Principality of Lüneburg, while Albert took the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-wolfenbuttel">Principality of Wolfenbüttel</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The salt city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a> was wealthy and largely independent, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-luneburg-succession">War of the Lüneburg Succession</a> in 1371 to 1388 the dukes moved their residence to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celle">Celle</a>. Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-i-duke-of-brunswick-luneburg">Ernest the Confessor</a> introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> from 1527. The principality was rural, with heathland, sheep and forests. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-william-duke-of-brunswick-luneburg">George William</a>, the last duke of Celle, saw his daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sophia-dorothea-of-celle">Sophia Dorothea</a> marry the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-i-of-great-britain">George I of Great Britain</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-william-duke-of-brunswick-luneburg">George William</a> died in 1705, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a> passed to his nephew and son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-i-of-great-britain">George Louis</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-calenberg">Calenberg</a> and was merged into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/electorate-of-hanover">Electorate of Hanover</a>. It later formed part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-hanover">Kingdom of Hanover</a> and is now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-luneburg.jpg" alt="Principality of Lüneburg" width="320" height="385"><figcaption>Image: Jürgen Krause · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DEU_Fuerstentum_Lueneburg_COA.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1269 CE – 1705 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Celle</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>John of Lüneburg, Ernest the Confessor, George William of Brunswick-Lüneburg</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism, then Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Low German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Brunswick-Lüneburg-Celle, Lüneburg-Celle</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1487&amp;focus=principality-of-luneburg" aria-label="Principality of Lüneburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4912 345 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Lüneburg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5232 601L5237 629M5232 601L5229 694M5232 601L5219 626M5232 601L5206 619M5232 601L5188 620"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5237 629h0M5229 694h0M5219 626h0M5206 619h0M5188 620h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5232 601h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5232 601h0"/></svg></a><figcapt